R = {
    2421: {
        "d": "A geodetic structure",
        "e": "The key word is 'some'. In a semi-monocoque fuselage, formers and stringers give the fuselage its shape and stiffness while the stressed skin carries part of the load, so the load is shared. In a pure monocoque the skin carries all of the load with no framework to help, a truss (girder) fuselage carries everything through a framework of longerons and bracing under a non-load-bearing covering, and a geodetic structure is a lattice of diagonal members.",
    },
    2424: {
        "a": "Caused by stretching",
        "b": "Caused by squashing",
        "c": "Caused by a sliding action",
        "e": "An airframe has to cope with five basic stresses: tension (stretching), compression (squashing), shear (one layer sliding over another), bending (a combination of tension and compression) and torsion, which is twisting. A wing is twisted when the ailerons are deflected or the centre of pressure moves, and the spars and stressed skin together form a torsion box that resists it without permanent deformation.",
    },
    2425: {
        "e": "A split flap is a plate hinged from the underside of the trailing edge: only the lower surface deflects, while the upper surface stays where it is. It gives a moderate increase in lift but a large increase in drag, which steepens the approach. A plain flap hinges the whole trailing edge, a slotted flap opens a gap for air to flow through, and a Fowler flap slides rearward before it deflects.",
    },
    2426: {
        "q": "To reduce the aerodynamic loads the pilot feels on the controls, balance tabs are designed:",
        "a": "To move in the same direction as the primary control surface",
        "b": "To be moved directly by the pilot's controls",
        "c": "To stay at a fixed angle whatever the control surface deflection",
        "e": "A balance tab is linked to fixed structure, so whenever the control surface moves the tab automatically moves the opposite way. The air load on the tab then helps push the surface in the direction the pilot wants, reducing the stick force. A tab that moves the same way as the surface is an anti-balance tab (it increases the force), a tab moved directly by the pilot is a servo tab, and one that stays where it is set is a trim tab.",
    },
    2427: {
        "a": "Move in the opposite direction to the elevator, reducing the stick force",
        "c": "Move in the same direction as the elevator, increasing the stick force",
        "d": "Return automatically to neutral whenever the elevator is moved",
        "e": "An adjustable trim tab is positioned by the pilot using the trim wheel and holds that angle relative to the elevator. Moving the elevator with the control column does not re-position the tab; it simply moves with the elevator as a fixed part of it, and only a new trim setting changes it. Tabs that move automatically, opposite to or with the surface, are balance and anti-balance tabs.",
    },
    2428: {
        "b": "One where the wing is supported entirely by internal spars, with no external bracing",
        "c": "One with two wings, one above the other, joined by struts and wires",
        "d": "One where the wing is mounted halfway up the fuselage",
        "e": "A cantilever wing is supported only at the root, with every load carried internally by the spars. A semi-cantilever wing is also braced by external struts running from the lower fuselage to the wing, as on many high-wing trainers such as the Cessna 172. The struts share the load, so the spar can be lighter, at the cost of some extra drag.",
    },
    2429: {
        "b": "Formers",
        "e": "A truss (girder) fuselage is a framework, usually of welded steel tube, in which the main lengthwise members are the longerons. Vertical, horizontal and diagonal bracing members join them into rigid triangles that carry the loads, and the fabric or light covering carries none. Formers and stringers belong to monocoque and semi-monocoque construction, and the main spar is part of the wing.",
    },
    2430: {
        "a": "The main spar",
        "c": "The wing root fitting",
        "d": "The leading edge",
        "e": "The rear (auxiliary) spar runs along the back of the wing and provides the hinge points for the trailing-edge surfaces, so the ailerons and flaps are normally attached to it. The main spar, near the thickest part of the wing, carries most of the bending load, and the leading edge is where slats or de-icing boots would be fitted rather than the trailing-edge controls.",
    },
    2432: {
        "a": "To increase longitudinal stability",
        "b": "To increase the stalling speed",
        "d": "To make the controls heavier in pitch",
        "e": "Longitudinal stability depends on the CG being ahead of the aerodynamic centre, so the tailplane has a long moment arm with which to correct any disturbance in pitch. Moving the CG aft shortens that arm, so the restoring moment is weaker: the aircraft becomes less stable and lighter on the elevator. An aft CG actually lowers the stall speed slightly, because less tail download is needed.",
    },
    2433: {
        "e": "Newton's second law says a net (unbalanced) force produces an acceleration proportional to the force and inversely proportional to the mass: F = m × a. Acceleration means any change in velocity - speeding up, slowing down or changing direction - so opening the throttle, braking on the runway and turning are all second-law effects. Equilibrium is the first law and action and reaction is the third.",
    },
    2434: {
        "d": "Increase the maximum speed of the aeroplane",
        "e": "A slat is a small aerofoil on the leading edge. When deployed it opens a slot that feeds high-energy air from beneath the wing over the upper surface, re-energising the boundary layer and delaying separation. The wing can therefore reach a higher angle of attack before it stalls, which lets the aeroplane fly at a lower airspeed. Slats do little at cruise angles of attack and do nothing for top speed.",
    },
    2436: {
        "c": "At the moment the wing stalls",
        "d": "Only when the flaps are extended",
        "e": "As the angle of attack increases, the stagnation point moves down and back under the leading edge, and the airflow lifts the stall-warning vane (or draws air through the reed). It is set to sound a few knots, typically 5 to 10 kt, before the stall, giving the pilot time to lower the nose. A warning at or after the stall would be too late to help, and the system works in every configuration, not only with flap.",
    },
    2439: {
        "b": "Move rearward",
        "c": "Remain in the same position",
        "d": "Move towards the wing tip",
        "e": "On a conventional cambered aerofoil, increasing the angle of attack up to the stall moves the centre of pressure forward, and reducing it moves the centre of pressure back. Beyond the stall the pressure distribution collapses and the centre of pressure moves rearward again, which is one reason the nose drops at the stall. Because the centre of pressure moves this way, a wing on its own is unstable in pitch and needs a tailplane.",
    },
    2440: {
        "b": "The ribs",
        "e": "The main spar is the wing's primary beam: it runs from root to tip and carries most of the bending and shear loads produced by lift, weight and landing. Ribs give the wing its shape and pass the air loads to the spars, and the rear spar carries the trailing-edge controls, but damage to the main spar is by far the most serious. Longerons are fuselage members, not wing members.",
    },
    2443: {
        "e": "A slotted flap moves down and slightly back, opening a slot between the wing and the flap. High-energy air from under the wing flows through the slot and over the flap's upper surface, keeping the airflow attached at large deflections, so it gives more lift than a plain or split flap. A Fowler flap also opens a slot but first slides well rearward to increase the wing area.",
    },
    2446: {
        "e": "A Fowler flap runs rearward on tracks before it deflects downward. The rearward movement increases the wing area and the downward movement increases the camber, and most Fowler flaps also open a slot, so it gives the largest lift increase of the common flap types. Plain, split and slotted flaps hinge close to the wing and do not add any significant area.",
    },
    2447: {
        "e": "Newton's third law states that for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. A propeller accelerates a mass of air rearward and the reaction pushes the aircraft forward; a wing deflects air downward and the reaction contributes to lift. Equilibrium is Newton's first law, and acceleration and deceleration is the second.",
    },
    2449: {
        "a": "Be more stable in pitch",
        "b": "Have a higher stalling speed",
        "c": "Have heavier elevator control forces",
        "e": "With the CG behind its aft limit the tailplane's moment arm is short, so the aircraft has little natural tendency to return to trimmed flight: it becomes unstable in pitch, the elevator feels light and it is easy to over-control. Stall recovery can be difficult because there may not be enough nose-down elevator authority, and spin recovery may be impossible. An aft CG reduces stability, stall speed and stick forces rather than increasing them.",
    },
    2451: {
        "e": "A plain flap is simply the rear part of the wing hinged so it can be lowered. Lowering it increases the camber, raising both lift and drag, but with no slot and no rearward movement it is the least effective type. A split flap hinges only the lower surface, a slotted flap opens a gap for air to flow through, and a Fowler flap slides rearward before it deflects.",
    },
    2454: {
        "c": "Former",
        "d": "Stringer",
        "e": "In a truss or girder fuselage the main loads are carried by the longerons: the principal lengthwise members, usually four, braced by struts and diagonal members into a rigid framework of triangles. The covering carries no load. Formers and stringers are the shaping members of monocoque and semi-monocoque fuselages, and the main spar belongs to the wing.",
    },
    2456: {
        "a": "The loads are carried by a framework of longerons and bracing, with a non-load-bearing covering",
        "b": "The fuselage is a lattice of diagonal members woven in a basket pattern",
        "e": "Semi-monocoque construction sits between a pure monocoque and a truss: formers (frames) and stringers give the fuselage its shape and stiffness, while the stressed skin carries part of the load. Because the load is shared, the skin can be thinner than in a pure monocoque, which saves weight and makes the structure more tolerant of minor damage. It is the most common construction in modern light aircraft.",
    },
    2457: {
        "a": "Formers and stringers give the shape while the skin carries part of the load",
        "c": "A framework of longerons and bracing carries all the loads",
        "d": "External struts carry the flight loads into the fuselage",
        "e": "Monocoque means 'single shell': the outer skin carries all the flight loads, with only light formers inside to hold its shape. With no load-bearing framework it can be light and roomy, but the skin must be strong and any dent weakens the whole structure, which is why most aircraft use semi-monocoque construction instead. A framework of longerons and bracing describes a truss fuselage.",
    },
    2458: {
        "e": "Even in a stressed-skin wing, where the skin shares the load, the main spar is still the primary load-carrying member: it takes most of the bending caused by lift and weight. The skin and spars together form a box that resists twisting, stringers stiffen the skin against buckling, and longerons are fuselage members rather than wing members.",
    },
    2461: {
        "e": "Ribs run chordwise from the leading edge to the trailing edge and are cut to the aerofoil section, so they give the wing its shape. They also support the skin against buckling and pass the air loads on the skin into the spars, providing strength as well. Stringers run spanwise to stiffen the skin, the main spar carries the bending loads, and longerons are found in the fuselage.",
    },
    2462: {
        "b": "A balance tab moves in the same direction as the control surface",
        "c": "An anti-balance tab moves in the opposite direction to the control surface",
        "d": "A trim tab moves automatically whenever the control surface moves",
        "e": "A servo tab is the only tab connected directly to the pilot's controls: the pilot moves the tab, and the air load on the tab then moves the free-floating main surface, so a large surface can be moved with little effort. A balance tab is linked to fixed structure and moves opposite to the surface, an anti-balance tab moves the same way as the surface, and a trim tab stays where the pilot sets it.",
    },
    2463: {
        "a": "Remain fixed while the stabilator moves",
        "b": "Move in the opposite direction to the stabilator",
        "d": "Move only at high airspeeds",
        "e": "A stabilator (all-moving tailplane) is very powerful and, because it pivots near its centre of pressure, can feel too light, making over-control easy. The anti-balance tab moves in the same direction as the stabilator, so its air load opposes the movement and the stick force builds progressively. A tab moving the opposite way would be a balance tab, which lightens the controls: the opposite of what is needed here.",
    },
    2464: {
        "e": "The horizontal stabiliser (tailplane) provides longitudinal stability, returning the aircraft to balanced flight in pitch. It does this by generating an upward or a downward force as required, depending on the position of the centre of gravity and the flight condition. It does not always lift the tail, and directional stability comes from the fin, while lateral stability comes mainly from wing dihedral.",
    },
    2465: {
        "e": "Newton's first law, the law of inertia, says a body stays at rest or keeps moving at a constant velocity unless a net external force acts on it. When the forces balance - lift equals weight and thrust equals drag in steady straight and level flight - the aircraft is in equilibrium and its speed and direction do not change. Acceleration is the second law, and action and reaction the third.",
    },
    2466: {
        "e": "A Fowler flap runs rearward on tracks as it extends, so it first increases the wing area and then, as it moves down, the camber. This gives the greatest lift increase of the common flap types, and the early stages add relatively little drag, which makes partial Fowler flap useful for take-off. Plain, split and slotted flaps hinge close to the wing and do not add any significant area.",
    },
    2467: {
        "a": "The centre of pressure is located forward of the centre of gravity",
        "c": "Drag increases when power is reduced",
        "d": "The wing's angle of attack increases",
        "e": "In most light aircraft the CG is ahead of the centre of pressure, so lift and weight form a nose-down couple. In steady flight this is balanced by the tailplane download and the thrust-drag couple, both of which depend partly on power. Reduce the power and the nose-up contribution falls, so the nose drops: a deliberately safe design, because the aircraft lowers its nose to maintain airspeed.",
    },
    2743: {
        "e": "A trim tab is a small adjustable surface on the trailing edge of a control surface. Its aerodynamic force holds the main surface at the deflection needed for the chosen attitude and airspeed, so the pilot no longer has to hold a steady pressure on the controls. It does not increase control-surface travel or change the aeroplane's limiting speeds: fly the attitude first, then trim out the remaining force.",
    },
    2759: {
        "e": "Mounting the horizontal stabiliser on top of the fin (a T-tail) usually keeps it above the wing's downwash and wake, and out of the propeller slipstream, in normal flight, so it works in cleaner, more consistent airflow; the tailplane also acts as an end plate that makes the fin more effective. The trade-off is that at very high angles of attack it can be immersed in the wake of the stalled wing, which can lead to a deep stall.",
    },
    2762: {
        "e": "Flutter is a rapidly building oscillation in which the aerodynamic forces and the inertia of a control surface feed each other. It is prevented by mass balancing - weights ahead of the hinge line so the surface's centre of gravity is on or just ahead of the hinge - and by keeping hinges and cables free of play. Trim and balance tabs reduce pilot effort but do not prevent flutter, and a more flexible structure makes it more likely.",
    },
    2763: {
        "e": "To roll left, the right aileron goes down and the left aileron goes up. The down-going aileron increases the right wing's lift and, with it, its induced drag, so the right wing is held back and the nose initially yaws right, away from the turn. This adverse yaw is why rudder is used with aileron, and why designers fit Frise or differential ailerons to reduce it.",
    },
    2764: {
        "e": "A Frise aileron is hinged behind its leading edge. When it is raised, its nose projects below the wing's lower surface into the airflow and creates drag on the down-going wing. That drag helps balance the extra induced drag of the lowered aileron on the other wing, reducing adverse yaw. Differential ailerons tackle the same problem by making the up-going aileron move further than the down-going one.",
    },
}
